theorem:
/-- This lemma claims that if an element `a` of the field operator free algebra belongs to both the bosonic and fermionic statistic submodules, then `a` must be zero.-/
lemma eq_zero_of_bosonic_and_fermionic {a : 𝓕.WickAlgebra}
    (hb : a ∈ statSubmodule bosonic) (hf : a ∈ statSubmodule fermionic) : a = 0 := by sorry

/-- This lemma claims that if a charge \( x \) is an element of the set of minimally allowing terms for the integer sets \( S5 \) and \( S10 \) and the potential term \( T \), then \( x \) minimally allows the term \( T \).-/
lemma minimallyAllowsTerm_of_mem_minimallyAllowsTermOfFinset {S5 S10 : Finset ℤ}
    {T : PotentialTerm} {x : Charges}
    (hx : x ∈ minimallyAllowsTermsOfFinset S5 S10 T) :
    x.MinimallyAllowsTerm T := by sorry

natural_question:
Let 𝓕 be a system of creation–annihilation field operators, and let normalOrderRel be the ordering relation used for normal ordering. Suppose φ and φ′ are operators in 𝓕, and φ satisfies the annihilation condition (i.e., 𝓕 |>ᶜ φ = annihilate). Then for any finite list φs of such operators, inserting φ′ into the normally ordered extension of the list obtained by appending φ is the same as first inserting φ′ into φs and then appending φ. In symbols:
for all lists φs,
orderedInsert(normalOrderRel, φ′, φs ⧺ [φ]) = orderedInsert(normalOrderRel, φ′, φs) ⧺ [φ].

Let F be a flux configuration satisfying the properties NoExotics and HasNoZero. Let S be a multiset of pairs of integers with exactly two elements, and suppose S is a sub-multiset of F. Then S must be one of the following seven multisets:
- {(1, −1), (1, 0)}
- {(1, −1), (1, 1)}
- {(1, −1), (2, 1)}
- {(1, 0), (1, 0)}
- {(1, 0), (1, 1)}
- {(1, 0), (2, 0)}
- {(1, 1), (2, −1)}.

Let F be a flux structure with no exotic elements and no zeros. Let S be a multiset of pairs of integers with exactly two elements, and assume S is contained in F. Then S must be one of the following seven multisets:
- {(1, −1), (1, 0)}
- {(1, −1), (1, 1)}
- {(1, −1), (2, 1)}
- {(1, 0), (1, 0)}
- {(1, 0), (1, 1)}
- {(1, 0), (2, 0)}
- {(1, 1), (2, −1)}.

theorem:
/-- This lemma claims that if two operators φ and ψ in the CrAnFieldOp structure of 𝓕 yield different statistics when applied to 𝓕, then the supercommutator of their corresponding field operators under the map ι is zero.-/
lemma ι_superCommuteF_of_diff_statistic {φ ψ : 𝓕.CrAnFieldOp}
    (h : (𝓕 |>ₛ φ) ≠ (𝓕 |>ₛ ψ)) : ι [ofCrAnOpF φ, ofCrAnOpF ψ]ₛF = 0 := by sorry

/-- This claims that for any two solutions S and T in the linear solution space of the PureU1 model of dimension n+1, if their values agree on the first n components, then S and T must be identical.-/
lemma pureU1_anomalyFree_ext {n : ℕ} {S T : (PureU1 n.succ).LinSols}
    (h : ∀ (i : Fin n), S.val i.castSucc = T.val i.castSucc) : S = T := by sorry

/-- This lemma claims that for any integer pair x in the list `allowedPairs`, the number of times x appears in this list is exactly one.-/
lemma mem_allowedPairs_count_one (x : ℤ × ℤ) (hx : x ∈ allowedPairs) :
    @List.count (ℤ × ℤ) instBEqOfDecidableEq x allowedPairs = 1 := by sorry

/-- This lemma claims that for any integer pair x belonging to the list allowedPairs, the number of times x appears in allowedPairs is exactly one.-/
lemma mem_allowedPairs_count_one (x : ℤ × ℤ) (hx : x ∈ allowedPairs) :
    @List.count (ℤ × ℤ) instBEqOfDecidableEq x allowedPairs = 1 := by sorry

/-- This lemma claims that for any two dependent pairs c1 and c2 of type (c : WickContraction n) × Option c.uncontracted, if their first components are equal and their second components are equal under an uncontracted congruence derived from this equality, then the pairs themselves are identical.-/
lemma extractEquiv_equiv {c1 c2 : (c : WickContraction n) × Option c.uncontracted}
    (h : c1.1 = c2.1) (ho : c1.2 = uncontractedCongr (by rw [h]) c2.2) : c1 = c2 := by sorry

natural_question:
For any Wick contraction on four labeled elements {0, 1, 2, 3}, the underlying set of contracted pairs must be one of the following:

- the empty set;
- any single pair among {0,1}, {0,2}, {0,3}, {1,2}, {1,3}, {2,3};
- any partition of {0,1,2,3} into two disjoint pairs, namely {{0,1},{2,3}}, {{0,2},{1,3}}, or {{0,3},{1,2}}.

In other words, the possible pairings are exactly the empty pairing, any single pairing, or any complete pairing into two disjoint pairs.

theorem:
/-- This theorem states that for any list of field operators φs, any Wick contraction φsΛ of length matching φs, and any two indices i and j in the finite set of uncontracted positions, if i is less than j, then the electromagnetic distance at the uncontracted position i is strictly less than that at the uncontracted position j.-/
lemma uncontractedListEmd_strictMono {φs : List 𝓕.FieldOp} {φsΛ : WickContraction φs.length}
    {i j : Fin [φsΛ]ᵘᶜ.length} (h : i < j) : uncontractedListEmd i < uncontractedListEmd j := by sorry

/-- This theorem states that for any list of field operators φs and any Wick contraction scheme φsΛ of corresponding length, an element selected by index i from the uncontracted list of φsΛ is a member of the set of uncontracted operators under that scheme.-/
lemma uncontractedListEmd_mem_uncontracted {φs : List 𝓕.FieldOp} {φsΛ : WickContraction φs.length}
    (i : Fin [φsΛ]ᵘᶜ.length) : uncontractedListEmd i ∈ φsΛ.uncontracted := by sorry

natural_question:
Let S be a charge configuration in (SMνCharges 1).Charges, and let j be any element of the finite set {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5} (i.e., j ∈ Fin 6). Then evaluating the function toSpecies at j, S, and the index 0 (together with the proof that 0 < 1) returns the value of S at j. In symbols:
for all S ∈ (SMνCharges 1).Charges and all j ∈ Fin 6,
toSpecies(j, S, 0) = S(j).

Let f: ℝ → ℂ be a function in the Hilbert space MemHS. For any real numbers b > 0 and c, the function
x ↦ e^{c x} · |f(x)| · e^{-b x^2}
is integrable over ℝ (with respect to Lebesgue measure).

Let f: ℝ → ℂ be a function in the class MemHS. Let b and c be real numbers with b > 0. Then the function
x ↦ e^{|c x|} · |f(x)| · e^{-b x^2}
is integrable over ℝ (with respect to Lebesgue measure).

Lemma (Static contraction after inserting a field operator with one uncontracted index). Let φ be a field operator, let φs be a list of field operators, and let φsΛ be a Wick contraction structure on φs (so its length matches that of φs). Let i be an insertion position in {0, …, |φs|} and let j be an uncontracted index of φsΛ. Consider inserting φ into φsΛ at position i and marking j as the partner (i.e., forming (φsΛ ←Λ φ i (some j))). Then the static contraction of this new configuration equals a product of two factors:

- If the insertion position i lies strictly before the position of j after accounting for the insertion (i < i.succAbove j), the factor is the supercommutator [anPart(φ), ofFieldOp(φs[j])]ₛ.
- Otherwise, the factor is the supercommutator [anPart(φs[j]), ofFieldOp(φ)]ₛ.

In both cases, this factor is multiplied by the original static contraction of φsΛ. Formally,
(φsΛ ←Λ φ i (some j)).staticContract
= {
  [anPart(φ), ofFieldOp(φs[j])]ₛ · φsΛ.staticContract, if i < i.succAbove j;
  [anPart(φs[j]), ofFieldOp(φ)]ₛ · φsΛ.staticContract, otherwise.
}

theorem:
/-- This theorem states that, for any finite collection S of subsets of indices (from a fixed finite index set) that is entirely contained within a larger set structure φsΛ.1, if a particular subset a belongs to the first component of the subContraction of S (under the condition hs), then a must also be an element of φsΛ.1.-/
lemma mem_of_mem_subContraction {S : Finset (Finset (Fin φs.length))} {hs : S ⊆ φsΛ.1}
    {a : Finset (Fin φs.length)} (ha : a ∈ (φsΛ.subContraction S hs).1) : a ∈ φsΛ.1 := by sorry

/-- This lemma claims that for any finite set \( S \) of finite subsets of indices, where \( S \) is contained in \( \varphi s\Lambda.1 \), and for any finite set \( a \) of indices in the complement of a certain subcontraction, if \( a \) belongs to the first component of the quotient contraction of \( S \) under \( hs \), then the image of \( a \) under the uncontracted list embedding map is also contained in \( \varphi s\Lambda.1 \).-/
lemma mem_of_mem_quotContraction {S : Finset (Finset (Fin φs.length))} {hs : S ⊆ φsΛ.1}
    {a : Finset (Fin [φsΛ.subContraction S hs]ᵘᶜ.length)}
    (ha : a ∈ (quotContraction S hs).1) : Finset.map uncontractedListEmd a ∈ φsΛ.1 := by sorry

natural_question:
Let x be a charge lying in the allowed set I.allowedBarFiveCharges ∪ I.allowedTenCharges. Then the following are equivalent:

- x allows the top Yukawa coupling, is not phenomenologically constrained, and is complete;
- x belongs to the set of non-phenomenologically constrained charges, denoted nonPhenoConstrainedCharges I.

Let n and m be natural numbers, and let f be a ℚ-linear map from the charge space of SMνSpecies with index n to the charge space of SMνSpecies with index m. For any charge S in the SMνCharges space of index n and any index j in {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, applying toSpecies at j to the image of S under chargesMapOfSpeciesMap f equals applying the composition of f with toSpecies at j directly to S. In symbols:
for all f, S, and j,
toSpecies(j, chargesMapOfSpeciesMap(f, S)) = (f ∘ toSpecies(j))(S).

Let f: Time × Space → ℝ³ be a time-dependent vector field that is differentiable. Fix a time t and an increment dt, and let i ∈ {1, 2, 3} denote a coordinate index. Then, for every x ∈ Space, the time derivative at t of the i-th coordinate function t ↦ (f(t, x))_i, applied to dt, equals the i-th component of the time derivative at t of the full vector field t ↦ f(t, x), applied to dt. In symbols:
for all x,
D_t[(f(t, x))_i]·dt = (D_t[f(t, x)]·dt)_i.

